1
00:00:00,090 --> 00:00:01,920
So now we come to the duties and

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:03,360
responsibilities of the board.

3
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:05,560
And here I'm going to be quoting copies

4
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:08,980
from the Companies and Allied Matters Act section

5
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:13,660
305 which describes the fiduciary duties of directors.

6
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:15,300
Who is a fiduciary?

7
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:18,020
A fiduciary essentially is somebody who is serving

8
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:21,040
the interests of others other than themselves, meaning

9
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:23,960
that their own personal interests are always subjugated

10
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:27,980
to those of the company that they serve

11
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:28,820
on as directors.

12
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:31,700
They also are expected to act in good

13
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:34,320
faith at all times and that duty is

14
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:36,160
owed primarily to all stakeholders.

15
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:40,300
I emphasise all stakeholders here because sometimes there's

16
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:42,200
a sense or a feeling that directors sit

17
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:44,700
on the board at the pleasure of shareholders.

18
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:47,400
That is totally incorrect.

19
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:51,640
Directors, whether they are non-executive directors, independent

20
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:55,440
executives, are there at the pleasure of all

21
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:58,360
stakeholders and they owe their duty to all

22
00:00:58,360 --> 00:00:58,820
stakeholders.

23
00:00:59,020 --> 00:01:02,200
And this stakeholder bucket is very large and

24
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:03,060
continues to grow.

25
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:06,260
So you have employees, you have regulators, you

26
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:08,480
have the society as a whole, you have

27
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:11,720
customers, you have everybody who has a stake.

28
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:13,500
That's why they're stakeholders.

29
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:16,080
And sometimes the stake that they have is

30
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:16,860
not very apparent.

31
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:20,840
So a civil rights activist, for instance, who

32
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:22,980
is not a shareholder, who is not an

33
00:01:22,980 --> 00:01:25,380
employee, who is not a creditor of the

34
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:29,600
company, can feel that the business is providing

35
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:31,860
whatever service it is at his pleasure.

36
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:35,840
And so such a person considers themselves to

37
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:38,420
be a social stakeholder and they believe that

38
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:40,880
their interest in the company gives that company

39
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:42,380
the licence to operate.

40
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:45,180
So as a director on the board of

41
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:47,140
a company, you have to be alive to

42
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:50,520
responsibility and acknowledge the fact that that duty

43
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:52,940
is owed to all stakeholders.

44
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,020
Directors are also expected to be responsible for

45
00:01:57,020 --> 00:01:58,320
delegated authority.

46
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:01,840
So whilst the board can delegate its powers

47
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:06,240
to, for instance, management, board committees, the board

48
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:09,259
remains primarily responsible for such delegated duties.

49
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:11,140
And it's not an excuse to say, for

50
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:14,060
instance, that we have a CFO, he's more

51
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:15,480
responsible for the accounts.

52
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:17,720
And so if anything goes wrong with the

53
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:19,180
accounts, we're not responsible.

54
00:02:19,780 --> 00:02:22,620
We delegate responsibly as a board and we

55
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:25,120
remain responsible for those delegated duties.

56
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:28,820
Directors are also expected to exercise power only

57
00:02:28,820 --> 00:02:29,820
for proper purpose.

58
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:34,140
So because directors have such enormous power, they

59
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:35,760
have to be careful as to how such

60
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:36,860
power is exercised.

61
00:02:37,180 --> 00:02:39,320
Otherwise, they can be sued for whatever losses

62
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:42,300
incurred if improperly exercised.

63
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:46,300
Directors are expected to exercise a level of

64
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:49,000
care and skill and ask themselves in all

65
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,120
situations, what will a prudent director do in

66
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:53,680
comparable circumstances?

67
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:57,500
Also, directors are not expected to make secret

68
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:00,820
profits, meaning that they shouldn't be involved in

69
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:03,660
contracts that are not fully disclosed to the

70
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:03,920
board.

71
00:03:04,580 --> 00:03:07,220
Because directors have access to lots of information

72
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:09,780
by virtue of their office, they come in

73
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:13,000
contact with a lot of confidential information, information

74
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,620
that they would otherwise not have come into

75
00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:16,360
contact with.

76
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:19,420
And this places on them a duty of

77
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:20,500
confidentiality.

78
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,100
They have to treat corporate information with the

79
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:29,900
utmost discretion and not share information with non

80
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:31,060
-members of the board.

81
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:35,440
One of the most significant responsibilities as well

82
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:37,540
is the duty not to fetter their discretion.

83
00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:40,520
And to explain this, it means that they

84
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:43,320
shouldn't pre-agree a position ahead of coming

85
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:44,760
to a board meeting, for instance.

86
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:49,520
So if directors are members of a particular,

87
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:53,860
let's say a venture capital business that has

88
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:57,000
invested in a company and they have entered

89
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,560
into shareholder agreements that require them to vote

90
00:03:59,560 --> 00:04:02,680
in a particular way, such shareholder agreements might

91
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:05,140
not stand the scrutiny of this provision because

92
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:07,240
what it means is that they have pre

93
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:10,960
-agreed a position which would not allow them

94
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:15,060
to exercise their discretion in the interest of

95
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:15,540
the company.

96
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:17,920
So they are reminded at all times not

97
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:18,940
to fetter their discretion.

98
00:04:19,459 --> 00:04:23,180
The final responsibility under this head is the

99
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:26,360
duty to not allow personal interests to conflict

100
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:28,440
with their responsibilities as directors.

101
00:04:29,320 --> 00:04:31,640
All conflicts of interest have to be disclosed

102
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:34,740
and not all conflicts of interest can be

103
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:35,940
cured by disclosure.
